# Release notes

`docs/release-notes.md` records what each tagged release changed, newest release at the top.
This skill writes one release's section, right before that release is tagged, from the PRs merged since the last tag.
Entries may be recorded ahead of time under an `## Unreleased` section, added only while an entry is waiting (a ticket that needs a note before its release); this skill reads them, judges them with the PR-derived rows, and moves them into the release section.
The person who invoked the skill (the repo owner, or the coordinator on their behalf) confirms every judgement call; the skill drafts and asks (SDLC-074: agents draft the notes and the commands, and never tag).

Always call this file and its sections **release notes**, never "changelog": a project's `CONTEXT.md` may already give that word a domain meaning, and one name avoids the collision either way.

Two readers, in this order:

- **Users** read the plain-language section. They know the product's own words (the terms in `CONTEXT.md`), not module names, PR numbers or config keys.
- **Integrators** (whoever deploys, operates, embeds or depends on this project) read "For integrators": contract, config and operational changes, and above all whether anything breaks.

The release procedure around this skill, including tagging, is the project's release-process document (SDLC section 9; by convention `docs/development/release-process.md`).
Where the project has none yet, say so to the person, treat it as an open gap, and use this skill's defaults (SemVer tag naming and the contract list the person confirms, both in step 5).

## Tracker shorthand

The `gh api` calls below use these shell variables; the epic numbers come from `.copier-answers.yml`, filled by `scripts/bootstrap.py`:

```bash
R=repos/{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}
PMR={{ post_merge_review_tracker or "<post_merge_review_tracker in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
HANGING={{ hanging_epic or "<hanging_epic in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
ARCH={{ arch_findings_epic or "<arch_findings_epic in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
```

## Ledger

Steps 2 to 6 fill a **ledger**: a scratch table (in the scratchpad, never committed) with one row per merged PR in the range and these columns: PR, title, ticket, epic or feature, `Requirements:`, `Door:`, labelled or legacy (with the release-note labels and the `Release note:` entry; a row from the running draft has the draft as its source and the issue it cites in place of the PR), area, user-visible (yes/no and why), verdict, line.
The verdict is one of *enhancement*, *bug fix*, *integrator*, *internal*, *netted*, or *folded into #N* (merged into another row's line).
The ledger makes every later judgement checkable: each line in the notes traces to rows, and each left-out PR has a verdict saying why.

## Labelled PRs and legacy PRs

A ticket carries release-note labels (`release:user`, `release:integrator`, `release:internal`, plus `breaking`) and its PR a `Release note:` field, re-checked against the diff before merge (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Release-note labels").
A project generated from TheForge has these from its first ticket; a repository that adopted them later has earlier PRs carrying neither, so a range may hold both kinds. Classify each ledger row first:

- **Labelled**: the PR body has a `Release note:` field and its ticket release-note labels.
  Each bullet in the field is one entry, and its prefix says where it goes: `Enhancement / <area>`, `Bug fix / <area>`, `Integrator` or `Breaking` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Release-note labels").
  One PR can give several entries in different sections; give each its own ledger row, keyed by PR and entry. A field reading `none` with `release:internal` is one internal row.
  The entry's issue number is the `(#N)` at the end of its bullet, not the PR's ticket; a bullet with no number is an exception (listed in chat with the PR number, never given a guessed one).
  Still run step 3's folding, step 4 (netting) and step 5 (the contract diff) over these rows: the field records one PR's view, and only the whole range shows a feature built across PRs, a change undone later or a contract the field missed.
  When the labels, the field and the diff disagree, the diff wins and the row goes on the "considered and left out" list as a disagreement.
- **Legacy**: anything else. Judge it with the full procedure below.

For labelled rows, "considered and left out" (step 7) lists only the exceptions: rows with labels but no field, bullets with no issue number, disagreements, and labelled user or integrator rows that were netted or folded. Legacy rows keep the full list.

## Procedure

0. **Find or file the release ticket, then run the queue sweep.**
   Search open and recently-closed issues for "Release vX.Y.Z"; the version is often not known yet, so a placeholder title such as "Release (pending version)" is fine, and step 5 renames it once the version is confirmed. File one if none exists (`Requirements: none (internal)`).
   Then run the queue sweep (`.agents/skills/queue-sweep/SKILL.md`), unless a report already exists for today's `origin/main` head, and post its report as a comment on the release ticket (SDLC-075, SDLC-101), not only to the owner in chat, which leaves no durable record.
   For each gap the sweep found that isn't simply a missed ticket (a ticket is its own record), once the owner decides to queue, defer or accept it, record that decision as its own follow-up comment on the release ticket, quoting the owner's words.
   Done when every such gap has a recorded decision; this step exists so a thread invoking `release-notes` directly, rather than through the release-process document, can't skip the sweep or its recording.

0b. **Gate on a reconciled threat model** (SDLC-076).
    Read `docs/security/threat-model.md`'s "Last reconciled against `main` @ `<sha>`" line.
    The reconcile must be at or after the previous release tag: the range's base from step 1 (the newest tag, `git tag --sort=-v:refname | head -1`, or for a backfilled release the tag before the one being backfilled).
    After `git fetch origin main`, `git merge-base --is-ancestor <base-tag> <sha>` must succeed: the reconcile sha must be the base tag or a descendant of it, not merely after some older commit. If it isn't:
    - run a full reconcile with `.agents/skills/security-docs/SKILL.md`'s **reconcile** mode against current code on `origin/main` (a docs correction, not a requirement change, so `.forge/requirements-driven-changes.md` doesn't apply); that skill is the one copy of the procedure, so don't re-derive it here;
    - land it as its own ticketed docs PR (`Requirements: none (internal)`, using step 0's release ticket) and get it merged before continuing;
    - re-read `origin/main`'s head before step 1, in case that merge moved it.

    Done when the threat model's reconciled sha is at or after the previous release tag.

1. **Fix the range.**
   The base is the newest release tag (`git tag --sort=-v:refname | head -1`); the head is the commit this release will be cut from, after `git fetch origin main green`: `green`'s current tip by default, or a newer `origin/main` commit the owner chooses (SDLC-111).
   For the first tagged release, the range runs from the repository's first commit (`git rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD`) to that head.
   When backfilling a release that is already tagged, the head is that tag and the base the tag before it.
   Counts and blame need full history: if `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` says `true`, run `git fetch --unshallow origin` first.
   Name both SHAs and the commit count (`git rev-list --count <base>..<head>`) to the person, with the next version as a placeholder until step 5 settles it.
   Done when the person has agreed the range.

2. **List every PR in the range.**
   Walk `git log --first-parent --format='%H %s' <base>..<head>`. A squash merge ends its subject with `(#N)`; a merge commit reads `Merge pull request #N`; a commit with neither gets its own ledger row keyed by SHA.
   Read each PR's title and body with `gh api "$R/pulls/<N>"` (REST works everywhere, including sessions where `gh pr` and its GraphQL calls are blocked).
   The body's template fields are the primary source: Before/After, `Ticket:`, `Requirements:`, `Door:` and blast radius. Where a field is missing (older PRs that predate the template), read the ticket and the diff's file list instead.
   Record whether each row is labelled or legacy, and for a labelled PR one row per `Release note:` entry ("Labelled PRs and legacy PRs").
   Past about 60 PRs, split the range into batches and give each to a subagent with the ledger columns as its output schema.
   Done when every first-parent commit in the range has a ledger row with every column filled except verdict and line.

   **Read the running draft.** Read every entry under `## Unreleased` in `docs/release-notes.md` into the ledger as its own row (source: the running draft, with the issue it cites); it goes through steps 3 to 5 like a PR-derived row.
   An entry is never dropped silently: it is kept, merged with the PR row it duplicates, or listed under "considered and left out" with the reason, and the person confirms.
   Done when every draft entry has a row.

3. **Judge each row.**
   A labelled row takes its verdict from its entry's prefix ("Labelled PRs and legacy PRs"); check it against the criteria below and record a disagreement.
   A legacy row gets a line in the notes when any of these hold:
   - it is **user-visible**: a user would notice it (a new control, a changed behaviour, a fixed symptom), whether or not it changed a requirement;
   - it touches a **contract or config** (step 5's list);
   - it is `Door: one-way` (a migration, a cutover, a deletion), which an integrator needs to plan for.

   Everything else is *internal*: `Requirements: none (internal)` work, refactors, tests, CI, docs, agent skills, and fixes to bugs no user could reach.
   Internal rows collapse into one closing count ("plus N internal changes").
   A row whose `Requirements:` line says internal but whose Before/After describes something a user sees is user-visible: the Before/After wins.

   Then **fold features**: PRs that build one feature (a design doc's slices, a feature and its follow-up fixes, the PRs under one epic that ship one capability) become a single line, referenced by the feature's issue or epic.
   A review pass's fixes (the post-merge review tracker {{ pmr_ref }}, or a stage 8 architecture pass) to a feature first shipped in this same range fold into that feature's line rather than appearing as bug fixes.
   Done when every row has a verdict.

4. **Net out what cancels.**
   A change introduced and undone inside the range is left out entirely: not reported as a fix, not listed as a known issue.
   The common shape is a bug fix whose bug was itself introduced after the base tag. Decide from evidence:
   - the fixing PR's ticket or body names the PR that introduced the bug; check that PR's merge is inside the range;
   - otherwise, `git log -L` or `git blame <base>` on the lines the fix changed: if those lines did not exist at the base tag, the bug never shipped.

   The same applies to a feature added and reverted, or a config key added and renamed, inside the range: report only the end state.
   Where the evidence is unclear, ask the person rather than guess.
   Done when every bug-fix row is either confirmed as fixing something that shipped at or before the base tag, or marked *netted* with the PR that introduced it.

5. **Diff the contracts.**
   Diff each contract the project's integrators consume between the base tag and the head (`git diff <base>:<old path> <head>:<path>`), then read the change, not just its size.
   Paths move between releases, so find each file's path at the base tag with `git ls-tree -r --name-only <base>` first.
   Each change becomes an integrator line, and a removed or changed (not merely added) item is a proposed breaking change.

   The list of contracts and where each lives is project-specific: read it from the release-process document.
   Where the project hasn't written one down yet, draft it with the person from rows like these, then propose recording it in the release-process document in its own ticketed PR:

   | contract (typical rows) | where it lives |
   |---|---|
   | Public API: endpoints, request and response shapes, or a library's exported interface | the route definitions, schema files or public module surface |
   | Events, messages or wire frames, both directions | the module that defines them |
   | Health, metrics and log shapes operators scrape | their handlers and the logging setup |
   | Container entry point and health check | the project's `Dockerfile` or equivalent |
   | Config keys, defaults and environment variables | the shipped config defaults and the config models that read them |
   | Embedding surface (props, events, methods) for anything a host application vendors | the project's integration guide |
   | Pinned dependency set | the project's lock and pin files, and any base image digest (SDLC-106) |

   In the pinned-dependency-set row, each moved runtime pin (not a dev-only one) becomes a "For integrators" line, and a major version move to a runtime dependency is proposed as breaking, like any other contract row.
   If the project publishes a "tested with" table for integrators, regenerate it from the head's lock files in the same pass, so it never drifts from what was actually tested.

   If the release-process document calls for an SBOM (SDLC-106), generate it here from a clean environment built from the head's pinned dependency files, with the SBOM tool the project pins in its dev dependencies.
   Step 9 hands its upload command to the person with the tag and Release commands; generating it is this skill's job, attaching it to the Release is theirs.

   Diff config as the set of keys and environment variable names, not as text: many keys exist only as model defaults, not in a shipped YAML or env file, and renames hide in PRs whose `Requirements:` line says internal.
   If the config loader refuses unknown keys, a removed or renamed key stops a deployment that still sets it: that is a breaking change.
   An added key, field or message type is an integrator line, not a breaking change, unless the project now requires it; a renamed or removed one, a changed default that alters behaviour, or a narrowed message type is breaking.
   Propose each breaking entry to the person with the diff that shows it; they confirm or drop it (SDLC-077).
   Then suggest the version bump by the release-process document's tag-naming rules, or by SemVer where it has none (MAJOR for a confirmed breaking change to a consumed contract, MINOR for a normal batch, PATCH for fixes only); the person decides.
   Once they confirm the version, rename step 0's release ticket to "Release vX.Y.Z" if it still carries its placeholder title.
   Done when every contract has been diffed and has either its proposed lines or "no change".

6. **Draft known issues** (SDLC-078). Sources:
   - **the previous release's Known issues, and any `## Unreleased` Known issues, as candidates only.**
     Never copy one forward unverified: look up its cited issue's state and close reason (`gh api "$R/issues/<N>" --jq '{state,state_reason}'`; `state` alone can't tell a fixed issue from a declined one).
     Closed `completed` with its fix inside the range: move it to Bug fixes (subject to step 4's netting).
     Closed `not_planned` (declined): drop it, or keep it only as a deliberate limitation citing the ADR or decisions-log entry that records it.
     Now covered by a Satisfied requirement: drop it;
   - open issues labelled `bug` that a user could hit (`gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=bug&state=open&per_page=100"`, skipping pull requests).
     `bug` is not a canonical triage label (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`), so it is usually sparse: most post-merge review sub-issues carry `ready-for-build` or `ready-for-structure`, and some bug-shaped issues carry no label. Treat its result as a partial list, never as "done";
   - to compensate, open issues labelled `ready-for-build`, `ready-for-structure` and `needs-triage` (`gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-build&state=open&per_page=100"`, likewise for the other two), and the post-merge review tracker's open sub-issues.
     `sub_issues` with no `per_page` returns only the oldest 30 and has no state filter, so paginate and filter: `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/$PMR/sub_issues?per_page=100" --jq '.[] | select(.state=="open")'`.
     Read titles for a user-reachable bug; most are process or review findings and will be judged not user-facing;
   - **hanging and accepted-risk items**, treated per `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Parking labels": each open issue a user or integrator could hit becomes a Known issues entry, and the rest are noted as not user-facing.
     Union three queries, since the `hanging` label, the `deferred-accepted-risk` label and the hanging epic's sub-issues can disagree (a label on a non-child, or a child with neither label):
     `gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=hanging&state=open&per_page=100" --jq ".[] | select(.pull_request | not) | select(.number != $HANGING)"`
     and
     `gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=deferred-accepted-risk&state=open&per_page=100" --jq ".[] | select(.pull_request | not) | select(.number != $HANGING)"`
     and
     `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/$HANGING/sub_issues?per_page=100" --jq '.[] | select(.state=="open")'`
     (without `per_page`, the default page size silently truncates). Dedupe by issue number before drafting; several items can fold into one known-issue sentence;
   - **count of deferred architecture findings**, treated per `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Parking labels": the open `deferred-arch-finding` issues make one summary line, "N further architecture findings are deferred rather than scheduled (epic {{ arch_ref }}). …", plus a one-sentence theme drawn from their titles.
     An issue listed individually elsewhere in this section stays listed, and also stays in this count; don't subtract it:
     `gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=deferred-arch-finding&state=open&per_page=100" --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request | not) | .number' | wc -l`
     (don't combine `--paginate` with `--jq '... | length'`, which counts **per page** and undercounts past one page; emit one number per issue and count lines, or use `--slurp`).
     Cross-check against `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/$ARCH/sub_issues?per_page=100" --jq '.[] | select(.state=="open")'`; the two should agree, and a child missing `deferred-arch-finding` is a labelling bug to flag, not to reconcile silently.
     Take this count as close to tagging as practical, since an issue filed between taking the count and cutting the tag makes the published number stale: re-take it if anything was filed in between;
   - for a backfilled release, the same queries with `state=all`, keeping issues created before the tag and closed after it (or still open); for the hanging and deferred-findings queries above, `sub_issues` has no state or date filter, so filter its `created_at`/`closed_at` in `--jq`;
   - accepted risks recorded on `main` and still true at the head: ADRs' Consequences sections, the architecture description's open gaps, the requirements specification's "Known gaps" section, and `docs/security/threat-model.md`'s "Accepted risks" section;
   - **every open row in `docs/security/threat-model.md`'s "Known gaps" table** (current as of this release, by step 0b's gate).
     List each Must/Should row individually, naming its tracking issue; fold Could rows into one summary line (count plus a one-sentence theme), like the deferred-architecture-findings count, unless they are few enough to list.

   **What a bullet cites.** Every Known issues bullet ends with an **open** issue or, for a deliberate accepted limitation, the ADR or decisions-log entry that records it; a closed issue is never the citation.
   A candidate with neither is not drafted until the person decides: either a ticket is filed for it (the person chooses its state) or it is dropped.

   Write each as one plain sentence about what a user or integrator may see, not about the code. The person curates this list; offer it as a draft.
   Done when every source above has been read and each candidate is drafted or noted as not user-facing.

7. **Write the section and show it.**
   First check every Known issues bullet against step 6's citation rule: for each `(#N)`, confirm `gh api "$R/issues/<N>" --jq .state` reads `open` (the ADR/decisions-log form needs no lookup).
   Fix any failure (a closed issue, a missing citation) before writing, and list it in chat if the person must decide it.
   Write the section into `docs/release-notes.md` above the previous release, in the shape below, then show the person, in chat:
   - the section as written;
   - **considered and left out**, one line each with the reason (in chat only, never in the file): for legacy rows, every netted row and every internal row a reader might have expected to see; for labelled rows, only the exceptions "Labelled PRs and legacy PRs" names;
   - the proposed breaking changes and version bump from step 5;
   - every question still open.

   The `## Unreleased` entries move into the written section (merged into the lines they belong to) and the `## Unreleased` heading is removed; the next ticket that needs an advance note adds it again.

   **If this project is itself a template source other projects sync from with `forge-sync`** (its release notes document changes *to* that template, as this repository's own `docs/release-notes.md` does), also write `docs/release-notes/<version>.integrator-steps.json` beside the section, one object per "For integrators" bullet (breaking and non-breaking), in order:
   `{"version": "<version>", "items": [{"id": "<issue number cited at the bullet's end>", "breaking": <true|false>, "summary": "<the bullet's text, with the trailing "(#N)" removed>", "refs": [<any other issue numbers the same bullet cites>]}, ...]}`; `refs` is optional and informational (the downstream check reads only `id` and `summary`).
   A downstream project's `forge-sync` ("Walk the integrator steps") turns each bullet into a checklist item rather than leaving the integrator to notice it in prose; a project that is not a template source skips this file, since nothing reads it.
   Omit it (never write an empty `items: []`) for a release whose "For integrators" section is "None."

   Done when the person has confirmed the section, the breaking changes and the version, and every question has an answer.

8. **Open the PR.**
   Every merge to `main` needs a ticket: use step 0's release ticket (`Refs`, since it covers the whole release, not just this PR).
   Cut a branch named for the change (`release-notes-vX-Y-Z`) from `origin/main`, commit only `docs/release-notes.md` (the new section and the removed `## Unreleased`, if there was one), and open the PR with the repository's template: `Requirements: none (internal)`, `Door: two-way`.
   The person merges it after review. Once you see it merged, post the merge's pending post-merge-review note on the tracker, in the form `.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`, "Pending reviews", gives.
   Done when the PR is open and its link is with the person.

9. **Hand over the tag and the GitHub Release text.**
   The tag goes on the merge commit of step 8's PR, so the tagged tree carries its own notes.
   A release tags `green`'s tip by default (SDLC-111), so either wait until `green` reaches that merge commit, or the owner chooses to dispatch CI against it and tag it ahead of `green`.
   `green` can lag `main`, so before waiting, ask the owner whether the Main Health thread should run a fresh local full-suite pass now (`.agents/skills/watch-main-ci/SKILL.md`, "Routine 2") rather than at the next scheduled run; the owner decides (SDLC-074).
   Print, for the person to run or approve:
   - the annotated tag command from the release-process document (or `git tag -a vX.Y.Z <sha> -m "<summary>"` where it has none), with a two-to-three-sentence summary of the release and its breaking-changes line;
   - the new section, verbatim, as the body of the GitHub Release for that tag, replacing whatever body a backfilled release already has;
   - if step 5 generated an SBOM, the `gh release upload vX.Y.Z <sbom file>` command for it, beside the tag and Release commands (SDLC-106).

   A backfilled release is already tagged, so only the Release body applies.
   Done when all of these are printed. Setting the tag, publishing the Release and attaching the SBOM are the person's actions (SDLC-074).

## Section shape

```markdown
## vX.Y.Z (YYYY-MM-DD)

<One or two sentences: what this release is about, in users' words.>

### Enhancements

#### <Area>

- <The new capability, key words in **bold**, near the start.> (#N)

#### <Another area>

- ...

### Bug fixes

#### <Area>

- <The bug as it was, in the past tense.> (#N)

### Known issues

- <What a user may see, and any workaround.> (#N)

### For integrators

**Breaking changes: none**

- <Config, contract or operational change, naming the key, message type,
  field or endpoint.> (#N)

Plus N internal changes.
```

- Headings: release, then the three user subsections, then "For integrators".
  Inside the user subsections, group by product area, from the project's list of user-facing areas (stage 0 writes it into `CLAUDE.md`'s "This project's own rules" section; the `Release note:` field's `<area>` uses the same list), plus another area only when a release's changes fit none of these.
  Omit an empty area; an empty user subsection says "None."
- The **breaking-changes line is always present** (SDLC-077): `**Breaking changes: none**`, or `**Breaking changes:**` followed by one bullet per confirmed break saying what changed and what an integrator must do.
- Every bullet, in every subsection, ends with an issue or epic reference in parentheses: the feature's issue or epic for a folded line, the bug issue for a fix, the ticket for an integrator change. Use the PR number only when there is no issue.
- Don't hard-wrap the file: each paragraph and each bullet is one line, and the renderer wraps it.
- Each bullet is one short sentence, readable without opening the reference; the whole section should read in a few minutes.
- **User entries raise awareness; they are not usage instructions.**
  Say *what* exists, not *how* to use it, unless the change in how was the point. Don't address the reader ("You can...").
  Keep detail and word count low, bold the key feature, and put the most impactful phrase near the start. Follow the product's own capitalisation for its named features, as `CONTEXT.md` writes them.
  - Enhancement: "Added **CSV export** of the overdue-invoice report. (#<n>)", not "You can now click Export on the report page and choose CSV to download the rows you can see."
  - Enhancement: "Reports can now be **scheduled** to arrive by email on a regular cadence. (#<n>)"
- **A bug fix states the bug in the past tense, not the fix.** "Large exports silently stopped at the first thousand rows. (#<n>)", not "Large exports now include every row." "The search box ignored a trailing space, so an otherwise exact match silently found nothing. (#<n>)", not "Search now trims trailing spaces."
- Known issues and the integrator section keep their fuller wording: integrators need the key, message type or endpoint and what to do.
- The date is the day the tag is set (for a backfilled release, the tag's own date).
- The first tagged release gets the same full treatment as any other (Enhancements, Bug fixes, Known issues and For integrators), drafted from the ledger the usual way, opening with one short paragraph saying it is the first tagged release and what it is, before the subsections.
